<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richborough</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rutupiae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richborough, Roman Rutupiae, is a site near Sandwich in Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where the Roman army landed in the invasion of 43 CE. It became a major port and supply base, marked in the late 1st century by a huge monumental arch, and in the 3rd century a fort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Shore. Its ruined walls are now in the care of English Heritage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richborough.jpg" alt="Richborough" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Des Blenkinsopp · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richborough_Castle_Entrance_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3295740.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Richborough lies north of Sandwich on the east coast of Kent, close to the Isle of Thanet, and is now some distance from the sea. From prehistory to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it stood at the southern end of the Wantsum Channel, which separated Thanet from the mainland and gave ships a safe route from the continent to the Thames estuary. Before the channel silted up, Richborough was an important natural harbour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until October 2008 it was uncertain whether the Claudian invasion had landed here, and some archaeologists favoured the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a>. That year two ditches dated to the Roman period were shown to belong to a defended beachhead protecting 700 metres of coast. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> withdrew, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> religious settlement occupied the site, following St Augustine's landing at nearby Ebbsfleet in 597. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">the Venerable Bede</a> the name Rutupiae had become, in his words, Reptacaestir.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when Dover and the other nearby ports proved too small, a large secret harbour was built at Richborough to supply the British Expeditionary Force with tanks, guns, locomotives, ammunition, horses and fuel. From 10 February 1918 three train ferries carried railway wagons and vehicles from there to Calais and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a>, the first sea-going roll-on/roll-off ferries used from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. By mid-1918 the site covered 2000 acres and handled 20,000 tons a week, and after the Armistice even more material came back through it in 1919 than had gone out in 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Roman site is managed by English Heritage. Richborough Power Station, opened within the port in 1962 and burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, then oil and finally Orimulsion, closed in 1996, and its three cooling towers were demolished on 11 March 2012. The settlement forms part of the civil parish of Ash.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richborough” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Roman army landed at <b>Richborough</b> in 43, and Kent was quickly absorbed into Roman Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/cantiaci">Cantiaci</a></li></ul></section>
